1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:04,710
Imagine a new developer joins your team on Monday.

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:10,909
They open their laptop and immediately spend two full days installing the IDEs.

3
00:00:11,109 --> 00:00:12,949
Not only this is inefficient,

4
00:00:13,109 --> 00:00:16,909
but it also creates inconsistent and unreliable setup.

5
00:00:17,069 --> 00:00:20,430
What if every developer could start with a fully configured,

6
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:23,469
perfectly consistent environment in seconds?

7
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:26,725
That's exactly what we're going to do in this clip. We're

8
00:00:26,725 --> 00:00:29,045
going to get into our Cloud Workstation,

9
00:00:29,225 --> 00:00:30,545
which we already created.

10
00:00:30,665 --> 00:00:34,865
Now, in this Cloud Workstation, which we have created in the previous clip,

11
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:38,505
I'm going to install a couple of softwares, and I want to

12
00:00:38,505 --> 00:00:40,865
make this as a preconfigured image.

13
00:00:40,944 --> 00:00:44,455
So in the previous clip, we have already installed the Google Cloud CLI.

14
00:00:44,575 --> 00:00:46,564
Now let's add a couple of more.

15
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:47,685
Let's say clear.

16
00:00:48,049 --> 00:00:50,700
Let's install kubectl. It is installing.

17
00:00:50,889 --> 00:00:52,250
It will take a little time.

18
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:56,049
Now once this gets installed, I'm going to install another library,

19
00:00:56,130 --> 00:01:00,689
let's say Helm, saying sudo apt‑get install helm,

20
00:01:00,849 --> 00:01:02,150
let's say clear.

21
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:05,989
Now let's say sudo apt‑get installed terraform.

22
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,160
Now this will install the Terraform.

23
00:01:08,529 --> 00:01:10,330
Now let's add a new file.

24
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:13,169
And I call it as a Dockerfile.

25
00:01:13,569 --> 00:01:17,199
Now inside this Dockerfile, let me type in one command.

26
00:01:17,489 --> 00:01:23,250
Say here, from asia‑southeast1‑docker.pkg.dev.

27
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:27,830
So I give that entire information, and now I'm going to create the

28
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:35,010
image for this saying in the console docker build ‑t dev‑image:v1.

29
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:37,699
And this will create one image for me.

30
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:40,459
Now once this image is going to get created,

31
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:44,860
this image I'm going to push to the artifact repository.

32
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,160
This image creation will take some time.

33
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:52,722
Once this is ready, we're going to use this image later on to

34
00:01:52,722 --> 00:01:56,902
create a new workstation so that all the new workstations which we

35
00:01:57,751 --> 00:02:02,722
create will have the same exact environment and all these specific

36
00:02:02,722 --> 00:02:04,522
softwares installed on that.

37
00:02:04,722 --> 00:02:07,402
Now you can see that it has been finished successfully.

38
00:02:07,482 --> 00:02:11,722
Let me just clear the screen, and now I want to push this

39
00:02:11,722 --> 00:02:14,623
specific image to the artifact repository.

40
00:02:14,723 --> 00:02:18,904
Now to push this specific image into the artifact repository,

41
00:02:19,044 --> 00:02:21,324
first, let's create an artifact repository.

42
00:02:21,404 --> 00:02:24,583
So I'm saying that gcloud artifacts repositories

43
00:02:24,583 --> 00:02:28,664
create workstation‑images repository.

44
00:02:28,723 --> 00:02:31,363
Now it will ask me that I'm not yet logged in.

45
00:02:31,444 --> 00:02:35,764
So let me just say gcloud auth login so that I will get authenticated.

46
00:02:35,884 --> 00:02:38,640
Now the authentication gets done, let me run the command again.

47
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:41,945
Now it is creating that specific repository.

48
00:02:42,126 --> 00:02:43,595
Now repository the got created.

49
00:02:43,845 --> 00:02:46,666
Now let's push the image into this repository.

50
00:02:46,925 --> 00:02:50,005
For this, I'm going to use the command docker tag,

51
00:02:50,285 --> 00:02:52,955
and then in that I'm going to give the dev‑image.

52
00:02:53,126 --> 00:02:55,966
So this is also giving me an error of unauthorization.

53
00:02:56,205 --> 00:03:00,705
So let me just do this, saying gcloud auth configure‑docker,

54
00:03:00,925 --> 00:03:03,106
and then I try to add the credentials.

55
00:03:03,647 --> 00:03:04,078
Say yes.

56
00:03:04,408 --> 00:03:06,307
Now let it run one more time,

57
00:03:06,488 --> 00:03:09,248
and now you can see that it did start pushing this

58
00:03:09,248 --> 00:03:12,248
specific image into the artifact repository.

59
00:03:12,298 --> 00:03:15,307
It will take a while to push this entire image.

60
00:03:15,527 --> 00:03:17,488
Once this image pushing gets done,

61
00:03:17,768 --> 00:03:21,848
we are ready to create the workstation using this image.

62
00:03:21,968 --> 00:03:23,798
Now this has been pushed successfully.

63
00:03:24,087 --> 00:03:29,770
Now I go back to my Google Cloud, and here in the terminal, I'm going to type

64
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:33,229
in the command saying create the workstation configuration.

65
00:03:33,649 --> 00:03:36,429
This time the name is dev‑custom‑config,

66
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:38,100
and most importantly,

67
00:03:38,410 --> 00:03:43,529
the container image we are providing here is the custom image that we

68
00:03:43,529 --> 00:03:47,210
had created and pushed into the artifact repository.

69
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:48,570
Now let's say Create.

70
00:03:48,759 --> 00:03:52,250
Now this will create the workstation configuration. And once

71
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:56,200
this gets done, we start going to create the workstation. Now

72
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,029
our configuration got created.

73
00:03:58,179 --> 00:03:59,919
Now let's create the workstation.

74
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:03,770
So for that, let me go down to the workstations here.

75
00:04:04,070 --> 00:04:09,250
Under the Workstation configurations, now I can see my dev‑custom‑config.

76
00:04:09,830 --> 00:04:14,309
When I go into my dev‑custom‑config, I can see the cluster name.

77
00:04:14,589 --> 00:04:17,928
And most importantly, under the Container image,

78
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:22,690
I can see the image that we had pushed into the artifact repository.

79
00:04:23,029 --> 00:04:24,269
Now on the top,

80
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:30,649
let's create on the workstation, and let's give a name to that workstation wk‑1.

81
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:35,399
And for this workstation, I'm choosing the configuration which we have created,

82
00:04:35,670 --> 00:04:39,269
that is dev‑custom‑config and say Create.

83
00:04:39,589 --> 00:04:43,029
Now it will take a while for this workstation to get created.

84
00:04:43,309 --> 00:04:44,029
Here it is.

85
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:48,339
We're going to click here on the three dots and say Start.

86
00:04:48,589 --> 00:04:52,559
It will take a while to start this specific workstation.

87
00:04:52,869 --> 00:04:54,079
Once this gets started,

88
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:59,540
we go inside this workstation and see that whether it has the Cloud CLI which

89
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:04,040
we already added is installed automatically there or not.

90
00:05:04,279 --> 00:05:05,959
Now our workstation got started.

91
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,059
Let me log in into this workstation.

92
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:09,640
Now let me open the terminal.

93
00:05:09,989 --> 00:05:11,160
And inside the terminal,

94
00:05:11,269 --> 00:05:15,160
let me check if our Google CLI is already installed or not.

95
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:20,399
We can just type in saying gcloud ‑‑version and say Enter. So we can

96
00:05:20,399 --> 00:05:23,239
see that Google Cloud is already installed here.

97
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:25,500
So we got all of those things installed.

98
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:29,299
So that's how to bootstrap secure, consistent,

99
00:05:29,519 --> 00:05:33,160
production‑ready development environments using the Cloud Workstations.
